1
00:00:00,078 --> 00:00:03,098
Speaker: You are standing in a
ballroom in Las Vegas, watching

2
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:04,708
strangers bid on your life.

3
00:00:05,488 --> 00:00:07,648
Not photographs, not memories.

4
00:00:08,447 --> 00:00:09,958
The actual objects.

5
00:00:10,648 --> 00:00:14,547
A horse and rider statue that once
guarded the entrance to your own theater.

6
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:19,368
A stagecoach, a country singer
built by hand for your wedding day.

7
00:00:20,148 --> 00:00:23,647
Numbered lots now, sold to
whoever raises a paddle first.

8
00:00:24,248 --> 00:00:25,757
Two thousand and fourteen.

9
00:00:26,457 --> 00:00:30,808
Burt Reynolds is seventy-eight years old,
and he is selling the evidence that he

10
00:00:30,808 --> 00:00:35,971
used to be the biggest star on Earth The
question people ask about Burt Reynolds

11
00:00:35,971 --> 00:00:37,971
is how he lost sixty million dollars.

12
00:00:38,622 --> 00:00:39,982
That is the wrong question.

13
00:00:40,641 --> 00:00:45,022
The real question is why a man who had
everything kept building the exact machine

14
00:00:45,022 --> 00:00:50,352
that would take it away from him, one
decision at a time, for thirty years.

15
00:00:51,072 --> 00:00:52,742
Start earlier than the fame.

16
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:56,091
Start with a hospital bed in 1957.

17
00:00:57,101 --> 00:01:01,701
Burt Reynolds went to Florida State on
a football scholarship, a halfback good

18
00:01:01,701 --> 00:01:05,491
enough that people expected something from
him before he ever said a word on camera.

19
00:01:06,211 --> 00:01:07,971
A knee injury ended one season.

20
00:01:08,442 --> 00:01:09,711
He came back anyway.

21
00:01:10,571 --> 00:01:14,872
Then a car accident took his spleen and
wrecked the same knee a second time, and

22
00:01:14,872 --> 00:01:19,371
the football career that was supposed to
define him was simply over at twenty-one

23
00:01:19,371 --> 00:01:21,761
years old, before it had properly started.

24
00:01:22,461 --> 00:01:24,871
Most people would have called
that the end of something.

25
00:01:25,542 --> 00:01:26,892
Reynolds treated it as a redirection.

26
00:01:28,111 --> 00:01:31,582
While he was recovering, a drama
teacher at a junior college in Palm

27
00:01:31,582 --> 00:01:35,962
Beach cast him in a play almost by
accident, and the same certainty that

28
00:01:35,962 --> 00:01:39,591
had carried him onto a football field
carried him onto a stage instead.

29
00:01:40,511 --> 00:01:43,061
He won a state drama award within a year.

30
00:01:43,591 --> 00:01:45,202
He never went back to football.

31
00:01:45,951 --> 00:01:49,931
That refusal to accept a closed door,
the same trait that would eventually

32
00:01:49,931 --> 00:01:54,371
cost him everything, is also the exact
trait that built him in the first place.

33
00:01:55,312 --> 00:01:59,221
A former stuntman he had befriended
along the way, a man named Hal

34
00:01:59,221 --> 00:02:03,031
Needham, would later hand him a road
movie script that nobody else wanted.

35
00:02:03,971 --> 00:02:06,461
That script became Smokey and the Bandit.

36
00:02:07,331 --> 00:02:11,611
Start the fame itself with the five
years nobody has matched since.

37
00:02:12,312 --> 00:02:17,861
From 1978 to 1982, Burt Reynolds
was voted the number one box

38
00:02:17,861 --> 00:02:19,201
office star in the world.

39
00:02:19,992 --> 00:02:21,262
Five years running.

40
00:02:21,992 --> 00:02:25,492
Not the highest paid,
not the most nominated.

41
00:02:26,251 --> 00:02:32,181
The one people actually paid to see over
and over, more than anyone else alive.

42
00:02:32,791 --> 00:02:38,432
It started with Deliverance in 1972, a
film so raw that people still flinch at

43
00:02:38,432 --> 00:02:43,611
one scene decades later, and it turned a
stuntman's son from Georgia into an actor.

44
00:02:44,362 --> 00:02:48,302
Five years later came Smokey and the
Bandit, made with his friend Sally

45
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:52,391
Field for a fraction of what a studio
film should cost, and it pulled in

46
00:02:52,392 --> 00:02:54,461
more than one hundred million dollars.

47
00:02:55,161 --> 00:02:59,521
He was earning four to five million
dollars a picture at a time when that

48
00:02:59,521 --> 00:03:01,491
number bought a small studio outright.

49
00:03:02,552 --> 00:03:05,552
He had the smile, the drawl, the timing.

50
00:03:06,102 --> 00:03:08,892
He had, by every visible measure, won.

51
00:03:09,792 --> 00:03:11,311
Here is the open loop.

52
00:03:11,982 --> 00:03:13,992
Keep the toupee maker in your head.

53
00:03:14,882 --> 00:03:19,532
You will need him later, because the
man who won that hard does not stop

54
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:22,211
moving, and Burt Reynolds did not stop.

55
00:03:22,901 --> 00:03:26,052
He poured money into a chain of
restaurants that promised steady

56
00:03:26,052 --> 00:03:30,382
returns and delivered none, a hole
that eventually swallowed something

57
00:03:30,382 --> 00:03:32,311
close to thirty million dollars.

58
00:03:33,241 --> 00:03:37,812
Then, in 1990, with his income
already thinning, he did something

59
00:03:37,812 --> 00:03:39,451
stranger than a bad investment.

60
00:03:40,251 --> 00:03:43,621
He borrowed four million dollars
from the television network airing

61
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:47,951
his new show, Evening Shade, against
money that had not been earned yet.

62
00:03:49,001 --> 00:03:50,051
The bet was simple.

63
00:03:50,522 --> 00:03:52,971
The show runs long enough
to sell into syndication.

64
00:03:53,222 --> 00:03:54,841
The syndication money arrives.

65
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:56,432
The loan gets repaid.

66
00:03:56,751 --> 00:03:57,802
Nobody notices.

67
00:03:58,312 --> 00:04:00,211
Evening Shade ran four seasons.

68
00:04:00,691 --> 00:04:02,381
Syndication needs five.

69
00:04:03,341 --> 00:04:06,451
The show was canceled one year short
of the number that would have saved

70
00:04:06,452 --> 00:04:11,101
him, and Burt Reynolds owed the network
three million seven hundred thousand

71
00:04:11,101 --> 00:04:13,871
dollars he no longer had a way to earn.

72
00:04:14,681 --> 00:04:17,971
By December of 1996,
the math had finished.

73
00:04:18,911 --> 00:04:22,431
He filed for bankruptcy with six
million six hundred fifty thousand

74
00:04:22,431 --> 00:04:26,152
dollars in assets against eleven million
two hundred thousand dollars owed.

75
00:04:27,131 --> 00:04:30,132
The list of creditors reads like
a cross-section of an entire

76
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:32,091
industry turning its back at once.

77
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:38,822
The Internal Revenue Service, Chase
Bank, his own talent agencies, and

78
00:04:38,822 --> 00:04:43,381
yes, the man who made his toupees
owed one hundred twenty-one thousand

79
00:04:43,381 --> 00:04:47,321
dollars, because even the illusion
of Burt Reynolds had gone unpaid.

80
00:04:47,861 --> 00:04:49,661
Picture that filing for a second.

81
00:04:50,171 --> 00:04:51,871
Not the number, the room.

82
00:04:52,472 --> 00:04:55,971
A man who once earned more per picture
than most people would see across an

83
00:04:55,972 --> 00:05:00,941
entire lifetime, sitting across from a
bankruptcy attorney, listing his own wig

84
00:05:00,941 --> 00:05:03,131
maker as a creditor he could not pay.

85
00:05:03,691 --> 00:05:05,241
The restaurants were a bad bet.

86
00:05:05,641 --> 00:05:08,642
The television loan was a bad
bet compounding a worse one.

87
00:05:09,391 --> 00:05:13,361
But the toupee bill is the detail that
turns a financial story into a human

88
00:05:13,361 --> 00:05:17,562
one, because it means the collapse had
reached all the way down to the smallest

89
00:05:17,571 --> 00:05:22,102
prop of the persona itself, the piece
nobody was ever supposed to notice.

90
00:05:22,831 --> 00:05:24,672
You would think the money was the wound.

91
00:05:25,092 --> 00:05:26,451
It was only the bruise.
